item.description:
Yuuko Bindings solves the fundamental problem of cross-device computing: "My files are on Device A, but I'm on Device B. Where are they now?" It gives every path (and file) the same UUID across devices, so app code never has to care about the actual path.For the full deep-dive (hash-based UUIDs, three-tier resolution, fallback creation, PhotonStars network layer, "How Yuuko Currently Works"), see <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="5 - Yuuko Bindings (Part 2)" data-href="5 - Yuuko Bindings (Part 2)" href="1-getting-started/5-yuuko-bindings-(part-2).html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">The File System Abstraction</a>.// On Device A
var (uuid, path) = await manager.GetOrCreateDirectory(@"C:\Music"); // Share just the UUID with Device B
// On Device B
var file = await Media.GetFile(uuid, "song.mp3");
// It just works, even if music is in D:\Songs on Device B!
See? It's elegant! You should be proud of this, dummy! Crosses arms smugly.
Note: I didn't notice she put this here, thanks<br>Related: <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="5 - Yuuko Bindings (Part 2)" data-href="5 - Yuuko Bindings (Part 2)" href="1-getting-started/5-yuuko-bindings-(part-2).html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Yuuko Bindings (deep-dive)</a> · <a data-href="XRUIOS Function Reference" href=".html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">XRUIOS Function Reference</a> · <a data-href="What is a XRUIOS" href=".html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">What is a XRUIOS</a>

How to start a new project that uses Yuuko Protocol, from nothing to a device that is up and showing a pairing code. Every step here was run on a brand new project, and the program at the end is the one that was tested.When it works, <a data-href="2 - Quickstart" href="1-getting-started/2-quickstart.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">2 - Quickstart</a> shows what to do with it and <a data-href="0 - All Functions" href="3-reference/0-all-functions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">0 - All Functions</a> lists every call.You do not install anything else. The Pariah and Eclipse DLLs it needs are in the repo's libs folder, and the packages it depends on (Zenoh-CS, YDotNet, Argon2, BouncyCastle...) are restored by the build.<br>On Linux, install libgdiplus too if you want app icons from <a data-href="5 - Apps and Icons" href="3-reference/5-apps-and-icons.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">5 - Apps and Icons</a>.git clone https://github.com/Walker-Industries-RnD/YuukoProtocol.git
dotnet new console -n MyApp
You end up with this (the names that matter are the three marked):Projects/ YuukoProtocol/ the clone YuukoProtocol/ the solution folder YuukoProtocol/ the library project &lt;- (1) YuukoProtocol.csproj libs/ Pariah and Eclipse DLLs &lt;- (2) PariahCybersecurity.dll ZenohNative.targets gets Zenoh's native library &lt;- (3) MyApp/ your project
Replace MyApp.csproj with this (three lines are Yuuko's, marked):&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt; &lt;!-- (3) Fetches the native Zenoh library for this machine on build and copies it next to your program --&gt; &lt;Import Project="..\YuukoProtocol\YuukoProtocol\ZenohNative.targets" /&gt; &lt;PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt; &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt; &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt; &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt; &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;!-- (1) The library --&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\YuukoProtocol\YuukoProtocol\YuukoProtocol\YuukoProtocol.csproj" /&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;!-- (2) SecureData, the type the passwords are in, lives in Pariah --&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;Reference Include="PariahCybersecurity"&gt; &lt;HintPath&gt;..\YuukoProtocol\YuukoProtocol\libs\PariahCybersecurity.dll&lt;/HintPath&gt; &lt;/Reference&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;/Project&gt;
Leave the Import out and the program builds but can't open a connection: the native library never reaches your output folder.This is Program.cs. It makes a device called pc, starts it, makes a pairing code, and stops.using System.Text;
using WISecureData;
using YuukoProtocol.Dogma; var Me = "pc"; //"phone" on the other device //Passwords are SecureData made from bytes (NOT SecureData.FromString, it wipes the string you give it)
var DeviceKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("this device's own password")); //locks the identity file, 8 characters or more
var ListKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("password for the saved device list"));
var FileKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("password for the reconnect file")); //Put the Start calls here: this runs once the node is up, and again every time pairing builds it again
Identity.NodeRestarted += async (s, e) =&gt;
{ await Reconnect.Start(); //paired devices find each other again after they were off //SimpleCRDT.Start(); OpTransform.Start(); HolyMother.Start(); await DAG.Start(RepoKey); Cogitarium.Start(); &lt;- whatever you use Console.WriteLine("Node is up. Connected devices: " + Identity.ViewAllConnectedMachines().Count);
}; //Made ONCE for a new group, then the same one is typed into every other device (see below)
var GroupKey = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(Identity.CreateGroupKey()); Reconnect.Open(Me + "-reconnect.json", FileKey); //BEFORE CreateNode
await Identity.CreateNode(DeviceKey, Me + ".id", Me, true, GroupKey, Me + "-devices.json", ListKey); var Code = await Identity.CreatePairing();
Console.WriteLine("Pairing code: " + Code); await Task.Delay(1500);
Identity.Shutdown(); //when your app closes
Reconnect.Stop();
dotnet run --project MyApp
You should see:Node is up. Connected devices: 0
Pairing code: YUUKO2-...
The code is long on purpose (about a thousand characters: it carries the address, a one-time token and a temporary certificate), so show it as a QR code rather than asking someone to type it.The first build takes a little longer because of the Zenoh download.Run it from a folder you chose (these names are relative to it). Back up the first two and keep all of them out of git.The names are yours to choose: DeviceID (the identity file), TrustedDevices (the device list) and the path in Reconnect.Open can each be a full path, and a name with no folder in front of it is the current folder.Same project, run on the other machine with one change: Me is "phone" and the group key is the one from the first device, not a new one.var GroupKey = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(TheGroupKeyText); //typed in, scanned or read out, never sent over the group's own network
Then one device shows a code and the other types it://pc
var Code = await Identity.CreatePairing(); //show it as text or a QR code
Identity.GroupChangeRequested += (s, e) =&gt; e.SetResult(true); //ask the user here instead //phone
await Identity.ConnectToDevice(Code, "pc", "phone");
<br>Check the fingerprint out loud the first time. More in <a data-href="2 - Identity" href="3-reference/2-identity.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">2 - Identity</a> and <a data-href="2 - The Group Key" href="4-security/2-the-group-key.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">2 - The Group Key</a>.Each part is one Start call, in the NodeRestarted handler above:Pairing builds the node again, so the Start calls and your own On(...) handlers have to be in the handler, not after CreateNode.Yuuko doesn't retry forever in the background. A call that asks another device can fail when that device is off, and a sync that stopped stays stopped until your app asks again. That's on purpose: your app decides when to try again.//A folder sync that didn't finish: ask again. Files that arrived stay done, a half downloaded one carries on where it stopped.
try
{ var Result = await DAG.ResumeFolder("pc", FolderUUID); //syncs, and if some files failed waits a second and tries again (3 tries) if (Result.Errors.Count &gt; 0) Console.WriteLine("Still not done: " + Result.Errors[0]);
}
catch (Exception ex) { Console.WriteLine("Couldn't reach pc: " + ex.Message); }
The usual places to do it:
When a device comes back: Identity.YuukoNode.PeerConnected += Peer =&gt; ... and call DAG.ResumeFolder for the folders you share with it.
On a timer, for devices that are on but unreachable.
Before you tell the user it is done: check DAG.LastSync[FolderUUID].Errors.
Two things do come back by themselves: paired devices that were turned off find each other again (Reconnect), and shared documents catch up (SimpleCRDT compares notes when a device connects and every 20 seconds).
Passwords: DeviceKey is 8 characters or more. The group key is 16 bytes or more and is kept like a password. Don't put either in your code or your repo.
Set HolyMother.Allow before other devices can open apps: until you do, it says yes to everyone.
The port: the node listens on one fixed port (saved in pc-reconnect.json, the first free one from 7447; if it is taken one day, another free one is chosen and saved). Allow it through the firewall on the local network. Reconnect (and Nearby) also open a separate plain link on a random port for their beacons, and nothing but beacons goes over it.
Same network: finding strangers (Nearby) and finding paired devices again (Reconnect) work on one Wi-Fi or LAN. Forming a Wi-Fi Direct group, or Bluetooth, is your app's job.
Files out of git: add *.id, *-reconnect.json, *-devices.json and .zenoh-native/ to .gitignore.
Platforms: Windows is what it has been tested on. Linux's app list and icons, Android and iOS have not been run.

SimpleCRDT (DOGMA I). Documents that every device can edit at any time, even offline, and that merge into the same result everywhere. There is no owner and nothing to resolve: use it for settings, lists and reminders.A document holds named text fields called labels. Changes are sent as they happen, and two devices that meet swap whatever the other is missing.More options on the sync itself, through SimpleCRDT.Start() (it gives back the DocSync)://On the pc
SimpleCRDT.Start(); var List = new SimpleCRDT.SharedDocs("pc", "Shopping list", DateTime.UtcNow, null);
List.CreateLabel("items", "milk\n");
await SimpleCRDT.CreateNewDoc(List); //every connected device gets it
List.AddToLabel("items", "eggs\n"); //and every change after this is sent by itself
//On the phone
SimpleCRDT.Start().DocReceived += Doc =&gt; Console.WriteLine("Got " + Doc.LabelName); //Later
var List = SimpleCRDT.ItemList.Values.First(d =&gt; d.LabelName == "Shopping list");
Console.WriteLine(List.ReadLabel("items")); // milk, eggs
List.AddToLabel("items", "bread\n"); //the pc sees this too
If the phone is offline and edits, then comes back, both sets of edits end up in the document. Nothing is lost.Use a label that is replaced each time. Create it on one device; the others receive it.//On the first device, once
var Settings = new SimpleCRDT.SharedDocs("laptop", "settings", DateTime.UtcNow, null) { UUID = "xruios.settings" };
Settings.CreateLabel("theme", "Whisper");
await SimpleCRDT.CreateNewDoc(Settings); //Any device: change it
SimpleCRDT.ItemList["xruios.settings"].AddToLabel("theme", "Midnight", Overwrite: true); //Every device: react to changes
SimpleCRDT.Start().DocUpdated += (Doc, From) =&gt;
{ if (Doc.UUID == "xruios.settings") ApplyTheme(Doc.ReadLabel("theme"));
};
If two devices change it at the same moment, the label ends up holding both values joined together. For a single value that must be exactly one, store the time with it and ignore the older one, or use a command (see <a data-href="7 - Commands and Devices" href="3-reference/7-commands-and-devices.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">7 - Commands and Devices</a>).Doc.CreateLabel("notes", "Hello world");
Doc.DeleteFromLabel("notes", 5, null); // "Hello" (null means to the end)
Doc.DeleteFromLabel("notes", 0, 2); // "llo"
Doc.NukeLabel("notes"); // empty, the label stays
Doc.NukeLabel("notes", DeleteLabel: true); // the label is gone
await SimpleCRDT.RemoveDoc(List.UUID); //removed on every device and can't be shared again SimpleCRDT.Start().AnyMemberMayRemove = true; //now any device can remove any document Never make a document with new Doc(). YDotNet's default ids collide about 6 in 100 times and two devices with the same id silently corrupt each other's text. SimpleCRDT.NewDoc() (used by SharedDocs) makes a safe one.
Text only. A label is text, not a list or a number: store those as text.
There is no "newest wins". LastUpdated is for showing.
A device that removes a document it never saw is ignored: nobody can block a real document by claiming it was removed.
Nothing is limited unless you say so. These are settings, on SimpleCRDT:SimpleCRDT.MaxDocs = 5000; //only if you want a limit
SimpleCRDT.MaxChunkedBytes = 1L &lt;&lt; 30; //allow documents up to 1 GB
A document can be any size. One message holds about 700 KB, so a bigger announce, update or push is cut into pieces (crdt.chunk), sent in order, and put back together on the other side. A hash of the whole thing is checked at the end, and pieces that don't fit together, claim to be more than they said, or come for a message that can't be sent in pieces are refused. One device can only have 4 of them being put back together at once, and ones that stop coming are forgotten after 2 minutes.A device can have any number of documents. The list of documents is compared in pages of about 200 KB, and the list of removed ones a page at a time, so the number of documents is not limited by the size of one message. The data that doesn't fit in one answer is sent after it.Things to know:
A big document is held in memory in full (Yjs keeps its history), so the real limit is your device's RAM, not these settings.
Pieces go to the devices you are directly connected to, and they pass on what is new to them.
Removed documents are remembered forever (so they can't come back), and that list grows.

HolyMother (DOGMA V). See what apps another device has, which are running, start and stop them, and get their pictures.Apps come from Steam, a WalkerWorks folder, and the apps menu (Start Menu on Windows, .desktop files on Linux). Another device sends an app ID, never a command line, so it can only start what this device listed itself.Each app has ID, Name, Source (Steam, WalkerWorks, StartMenu or Desktop) and Running.HolyMother.Start(); var Apps = await HolyMother.GetApps("pc");
foreach (var App in Apps) Console.WriteLine($"{App.Name} ({App.Source}) {(App.Running ? "running" : "")}"); var Spotify = Apps.First(a =&gt; a.Name == "Spotify");
await HolyMother.StartApp("pc", Spotify.ID); var Open = await HolyMother.GetRunning("pc");
await HolyMother.StopApp("pc", Spotify.ID);
The default allows everything from any device in the group. Say no to block it:HolyMother.Allow = (Device, Action) =&gt; Device == "phone" &amp;&amp; Action == "start"; //Action is "start" or "stop"
HolyMother.Allow = (Device, Action) =&gt; false; //nobody
Give a name, an ID, or the path of an app, one or a list:var Icon = await HolyMother.GetIcon("pc", "Spotify"); //bytes of a PNG
var Icons = await HolyMother.GetIcons("pc", new[] { "Spotify", "Steam", Apps[3].ID }); //name -&gt; bytes, the ones with no picture are left out
var Mine = HolyMother.LocalIcon("Spotify"); //this device File.WriteAllBytes("spotify.png", Icon);
Lists are queued: if you ask for several lists at once they go one at a time in the order they came, and a long list is asked for in batches so each answer fits in a command. A picture you already fetched isn't asked for again.On Windows the picture is the program's icon as a PNG. On Linux it's the icon file (a PNG, or an SVG). Check the first bytes if you need to know which.Another device can only ask about apps this device listed. A path only works if it's the path of one of them, so it can't be used to look at other files.var Here = HolyMother.LocalApps();
foreach (var App in Here) Console.WriteLine(App.Name); var Problem = HolyMother.StartLocal(Here[0].ID); //null means it worked
if (Problem != null) Console.WriteLine(Problem); Stopping asks the app to close, and ends it after 2 seconds. It matches by program, so it closes every copy of that program.
A Start Menu shortcut that has arguments (it runs through powershell, cmd or a browser) and a Linux entry that runs through flatpak, snap or env can be started but not stopped: we can't tell which process is theirs.
The default of Allow is "yes". Set it before you let any device control apps.
WalkerWorks apps: a folder per app, the app is the file named like the folder (Name.exe, Name.xur, or Name).

Identity makes this device's node and handles who is connected. Everything else starts from here.Events: GroupChangeRequested (a device wants to add or remove another, you decide), NodeRestarted (pairing built the node again), ConnectionStatusChange (a kick or ban happened).//Both of these are made once, kept, and the same group key is used on EVERY device
var DeviceKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("this device's own password")); //locks the identity file, 8 characters or more. NOT SecureData.FromString, it wipes the string
var ListKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("password for the saved device list"));
var GroupKey = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("the group key"); //Identity.CreateGroupKey() makes one //DeviceID is the file this device's identity is kept in, DeviceName is what the others call it
await Identity.CreateNode(DeviceKey, "pc.id", "pc", true, GroupKey, "pc-devices.json", ListKey);
Password locks the identity file (this device's private keys):
A new identity is saved encrypted, and an identity file from before there was a password is locked the first time it is loaded with one (it stays the same device).
It has to be 8 characters or more. A shorter one, or none, throws.
A wrong password throws and the file is left as it was.
Keep it separate from the user's own password, and keep it somewhere safe: without it the identity can't be opened, and the device would have to pair again.
ListensToOthers isn't used yet (the node always listens on a free port).//On the pc: show this
var Code = await Identity.CreatePairing();
Console.WriteLine(Code); // YUUKO2-... //On the phone: type it in (DeviceName is this device, DeviceNickname is what you call the other one)
await Identity.ConnectToDevice(Code, "phone", "pc");
Pairing builds the node again, so run your start-up again afterwards:Identity.NodeRestarted += (s, e) =&gt;
{ SimpleCRDT.Start(); OpTransform.Start(); HolyMother.Start(); //...and register your own commands again
};
The code only works for a few minutes and once. CancelConnecting() closes the window early.foreach (var Name in Identity.ViewAllConnectedMachines())
{ var Ping = await Identity.GetLatency(Name); Console.WriteLine($"{Name}: {Ping.TotalMilliseconds:F0} ms, recent activity: {await Identity.HasRecentActivity(Name)}");
}
With nobody listening, group changes are refused. A question left for a minute counts as no.Identity.GroupChangeRequested += async (sender, e) =&gt;
{ var Action = e.IsIntroduction ? "trust" : "stop trusting"; bool Yes = await YourPopup.ShowAsync($"{e.IntroducedBy} wants you to {Action} {e.DeviceId}. Allow?"); e.SetResult(Yes);
}; await Identity.TrustDevice("tablet"); //asks the other devices to trust it too
await Identity.UntrustDevice("tablet");
await Identity.KickDevice("tablet", Ban: false, Reason: "testing"); //it can come back
await Identity.KickDevice("tablet", Ban: true, Reason: "lost device");
Identity.UnbanDevice("tablet");
Identity.Shutdown();
Without this, two paired devices that were turned off have to pair again: the address of the other device was only remembered in memory, and the port could change. Reconnect fixes that.Reconnect.Open("reconnect.json", FileKey); //BEFORE CreateNode: loads (or makes) this device's UUID, its port and the paired devices
await Identity.CreateNode(...);
await Reconnect.Start(); //after CreateNode, and again in NodeRestarted like the other Start calls

The layer underneath everything else: your own commands between devices, the list of paired devices, and pairing codes. Identity.YuukoNode is the node (an EclipseBroker).Use it when none of the other pages fit: copying a clipboard, handing music to another device, any "do this once on that device".A command is at most 1 MB. A command that is sent twice by mistake (a retry) only runs once on the other device.//On the pc: answer it
Identity.YuukoNode.On("hello", (Context, Cancel) =&gt; Task.FromResult(CommandResult.Ok("hi " + Context.Origin + ", you sent " + Context.PayloadAsString()))); //On the phone: send it
var Ack = await Identity.YuukoNode.SendCommandAsync("pc", "hello", Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("test")); if (Ack.IsOk) Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(Ack.Result)); //hi phone, you sent test
else Console.WriteLine($"{Ack.Status}: {Ack.Message}");
Context.Origin is the device that started it. It is checked, a device can't pretend to be another.//Sending
await Identity.YuukoNode.BroadcastAsync("clipboard.set", Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(Text)); //Receiving
Identity.YuukoNode.On("clipboard.set", (Context, Cancel) =&gt;
{ if (Context.Payload.Length &lt; 100_000) SystemClipboard.Set(Context.PayloadAsString()); return Task.FromResult(CommandResult.Ok());
});
Don't send a clipboard change that you only just received, or two devices pass it back and forth forever.Identity.YuukoNode.On("shutdown", (Context, Cancel) =&gt; Task.FromResult(Context.Origin == "pc" ? CommandResult.Ok() : CommandResult.Denied("only the pc may")));
var Node = Identity.YuukoNode; foreach (var Status in Node.DeviceStatuses()) Console.WriteLine($"{Status.Moniker}: online={Status.IsOnline}"); await Node.Device("pc").SendTextAsync("hello", "hi"); var Acks = await Node.AllDevices().OnlineOnly().SendAsync("hello", Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("hi")); //name -&gt; answer
foreach (var (Name, Ack) in Acks) Console.WriteLine($"{Name}: {Ack.Status}"); Node.PeerConnected += Peer =&gt; Console.WriteLine(Peer.Id + " connected");
Node.Devices.SetGroups("phone", new[] { "phones", "mobile" });
Node.Devices.SetGroups("tablet", new[] { "mobile" }); await Node.DeviceGroup("mobile").SendAsync("notice", Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("hello")); Your command names should have a prefix so they don't clash (clipboard., music.). The library's own are crdt., ot., holy., dag..
Handlers run on a background thread and may take a while, but not forever (there's a time limit).
A command that throws becomes an error answer that names only the exception type, never the message.
Pairing builds the node again, so commands you registered with On are gone: register them again after Identity.NodeRestarted.
Audit what a device decided with <a data-href="9 - Audit Log" href="3-reference/9-audit-log.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">9 - Audit Log</a>.

To start developing with Yuuko Protocol, you start with creating a node. This is what all other programs on the computer will use to communicate with other devices through Yuuko Protocol.For this example, let's assume you want to connect a phone and computer.using System.Text;
using WISecureData;
using YuukoProtocol.Dogma; var Me = "pc"; //"phone" on the phone //DeviceKey locks this device's identity file (8 characters or more), the others lock the saved device list and the folder repository
var DeviceKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("this device's own password")); //NOT SecureData.FromString, it wipes the string
var ListKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("password for the saved device list"));
var RepoKey = new SecureData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("password for the folder repository")); //The group key is made once, on the first device, and the same one is typed into the others
var GroupKey = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(Identity.CreateGroupKey()); //Run all the Start calls here, because pairing builds the node again (CreateNode raises this once too)
Identity.NodeRestarted += async (s, e) =&gt;
{ SimpleCRDT.Start(); OpTransform.Start(); HolyMother.Start(); await DAG.Start(RepoKey); Cogitarium.Start(); //your paired devices (use Nearby.Start() later for strangers) //Your own commands also have to be registered again Identity.YuukoNode.On("hello", (Context, _) =&gt; Task.FromResult(CommandResult.Ok(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("hi " + Me))));
}; //Only a device you control may start or stop apps
var CanControl = new HashSet&lt;string&gt; { "phone", "tablet" };
HolyMother.Allow = (Device, Action) =&gt; CanControl.Contains(Device); await Identity.CreateNode(DeviceKey, Me + ".id", Me, true, GroupKey, Me + "-devices.json", ListKey); //You should optimally save the groupkey so you can use it later, but do so per session
Great, now how do you connect your PC to your phone? Your PC will show a code and the phone will be what has to type it.//PC
var Code = await Identity.CreatePairing();
Console.WriteLine(Code); //show it as text or a QR code
Identity.GroupChangeRequested += (s, e) =&gt; e.SetResult(true); //ask the user here instead //Phone
await Identity.ConnectToDevice(Code, "pc", "phone");
Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", Identity.ViewAllConnectedMachines())); //pc
For our first example, let's create a shopping list on our laptop and have it sync to the phone.//PC
var List = new SimpleCRDT.SharedDocs("pc", "Shopping list", DateTime.UtcNow, null);
List.CreateLabel("items", "TSF Rated Paint x10\n");
await SimpleCRDT.CreateNewDoc(List); //the phone gets it
List.AddToLabel("items", "TSF Replacement Arm x2\n"); //and every change after this //Phone
SimpleCRDT.Start().DocUpdated += (Doc, From) =&gt; Console.WriteLine(Doc.ReadLabel("items"));
Now you have a shopping list but you want to have both devices edit a note at the same time.//PC (the owner, only the phone may edit)
var Notes = OpTransform.CreateText("notes", "Ideas:\n", new List&lt;string&gt; { "phone" }); //Phone
var Notes = await OpTransform.JoinText("pc", "notes");
Notes.Insert(Notes.Text.Length, "- add Hakodate to the tour\n");
await Notes.WaitUntilSynced(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
Notes.Changed += (Text, Remote, Author) =&gt; Console.WriteLine(Text);
Now let's open an app on our laptop from our phone!//Phone
try
{ var Apps = await HolyMother.GetApps("pc"); var Spotify = Apps.First(a =&gt; a.Name == "Spotify"); await HolyMother.StartApp("pc", Spotify.ID); //throws if the PC refused or is off
}
catch (Exception ex) { Console.WriteLine("Couldn't: " + ex.Message); }
Let's have a folder sync between the phone and laptop now!//PC
var Folder = await DAG.ShareFolder(@"D:\Movies", new List&lt;string&gt; { "phone" }); //Phone: the same UUID, its own path
await DAG.JoinFolder(Folder.UUID, "/sdcard/Movies", new List&lt;string&gt; { "pc" });
var Result = await DAG.SyncFolder("pc", Folder.UUID);
Console.WriteLine($"{Result.Downloaded} new, {Result.Conflicts.Count} conflicts");
Using the meshnet system, let's stream data to the phone!//PC is the source
Cogitarium.JoinChunk("geofront");
var Log = Cogitarium.StartStream("geofront", "Unit-02 vs Angel");
Console.WriteLine("Give the phone this: " + Cogitarium.MyFingerprint); await Log.Send(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("Asuka: Progressive knife, going in"));
await Log.Send(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("Angel: AT Field neutralized"));
Console.WriteLine($"{Log.Coverage:P0} of the devices are green"); //Phone: tell the PC how it's doing (so the best get pieces first), accept the PC, and read
Cogitarium.JoinChunk("geofront");
Cogitarium.MyStatus = new Cogitarium.DeviceStatus { WifiStrength = 80, WifiUsage = 25, BatteryPercent = 70, BatteryCapacity = 4000 };
Cogitarium.AcceptSource(FingerprintFromThePC);
Cogitarium.SegmentReceived += (Stream, Piece) =&gt; Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(Piece.Data)); //Weak mesh? Get it straight from the PC instead
await Cogitarium.SetRoute("geofront", Cogitarium.Route.Auto);
When you want to stop using Yuuko Protocol, simply doIdentity.Shutdown();
You can take an older device

OpTransform (DOGMA II). Several devices typing into one piece of text at once, like a notes app or a script. Everyone ends with the same text and nothing typed is lost.One device owns each text: the one that created it. It puts everybody's edits in order. Other devices join by the owner's name. You never deal with revisions or sequence numbers; that is all inside.OpTransform.Start(); //Create (on the owner)
var Notes = OpTransform.CreateText("notes", "Shopping list:\n"); //Join (on every other device)
var Notes = await OpTransform.JoinText("pc", "notes"); //Edit
Notes.Insert(Notes.Text.Length, "- milk\n"); //Read
Console.WriteLine(Notes.Text);
Notes.Changed += (Text, Remote, Author) =&gt; { if (Remote) ui.Show(Text); }; //Author says who //Sync
await Notes.WaitUntilSynced(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); //"the owner has confirmed my edits"
A text box gives you the whole new text, not an edit. SetText works out the smallest edit:textBox.TextChanged += (s, e) =&gt; Notes.SetText(textBox.Text);
Notes.Changed += (Text, Remote, Author) =&gt; { if (Remote) textBox.Text = Text; };
var Rules = OpTransform.CreateText("rules", "House rules\n", Writers: new[] { "phone" }); //On a device that is not on the list
var Rules = await OpTransform.JoinText("pc", "rules");
Rules.Rejected += Why =&gt; Console.WriteLine("The owner said no: " + Why);
Rules.Insert(0, "my own rule\n"); //refused, the text goes back to the owner's
foreach (var Name in OpTransform.TextList) Console.WriteLine(Name); var Same = OpTransform.GetText("notes");
OpTransform.RemoveText("notes"); If the owner is offline, nobody's edits are confirmed. They are kept and sent when it comes back. There is no election of a new owner. Pick the device that is on most as the owner.
A device that was away catches up when it reconnects and keeps what it typed meanwhile.
An edit that would cut an emoji in half is refused (JSON can't carry half of one).
A text is at most 200,000 characters.
JoinText throws if the owner isn't connected or doesn't have a text with that name.
Pairing builds the node again: after Identity.NodeRestarted, call OpTransform.Start() again and join again.

Each node is a peer-to-peer Zenoh participant that establishes an Eclipse-secured session per peer before any data moves. All application crypto runs through the Eclipse DLL (EclipseProject.Security) and Pariah (post-quantum primitives) — no reimplementation.Handshake (challenge-response over a handshake topic):
Requester → HELLO { kyberPublicKey, nonceC }
Provider runs an admission check (banned-list today; trust levels later)
Provider Kyber-encapsulates to the requester's key, derives session keys via Security.PrepareKeys (HKDF-SHA256), and → CHALLENGE { kemCipher, nonceS, sessionId, enc(random) }
Requester decapsulates (proving it holds the private key), derives the same keys, decrypts the challenge and echoes it back encrypted
Failure → provider erases the pending handshake and → REJECT
Success → both hold a HandshakeSession; data flows encrypted end-to-end
Sessions &amp; lanes: each session multiplexes over a pool of independent AeadChannel lanes (default 8) with distinct nonce spaces, so multiple requests to one peer run in parallel while each lane stays sequence-ordered. Lane 0 doubles as the handshake lane.Beyond the two other libraries mentioned in Welcome, you want to add
dotnet add package Zenoh-CS --version 0.4.1
This is a P/Invoke Wrapper over Zenoh-c, meaning you should be getting most of the benefits of Zenoh. The official Zenoh has yet to make an official binding.
Zenoh-CS 0.4.1 requires zenoh-c v1.6.2 built/installed with the UNSTABLE_API and SHARED_MEMORY cmake options, matching the flags baked into the Zenoh-CS managed assembly. Grab a prebuilt release from github.com/eclipse-zenoh/zenoh-c/releases or build from source, and make sure libzenohc(.so/.dll/.dylib) is on the loader path (next to the executable, or on PATH/LD_LIBRARY_PATH).using YuukoProtocol; // 1. Transport: Zenoh in production, LocalBroker for tests.
IYuukoApi transport = ZenohBroker.Open(); // or new LocalBroker() // 2. Give this node a post-quantum identity.
var identity = YuukoIdentity.Create("desktop-001"); // 3. Wrap the transport in the Eclipse-secured overlay.
var opts = new EclipseBrokerOptions();
opts.Handshake.BannedClients.Add("known-bad-app"); // admission
opts.Handshake.MaxChannels = 8; // parallel lanes per peer
using var node = EclipseBroker.Create(transport, identity, opts);
await node.StartAsync(); // PROVIDER: publish data others can request.
node.Serve("yuuko/vectorgear/sensors", YuukoPacket.FromBytes("temp", System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("36.5C"))); // REQUESTER: handshake-on-first-use, then fetch encrypted end-to-end.
var packets = await node.RequestAsync("desktop-001", "yuuko/vectorgear/sensors");
var testPacketGeneric = YuukoPacket.FromBytes("XM3", Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("The XM3 is a revolutionary operating system designed for the Tactical Surface Fighters (TSFs). It drastically improves the mechas' combat capabilities and significantly reduces pilot fatality rates by optimizing movement, handling data dynamically, and utilizing advanced flight mechanics.")); //Generic tests
//Basic packet test Console.WriteLine("Creating Broker...");
Console.WriteLine("Packet Test"); var BasicPacketBroker = new LocalBroker(); await BasicPacketBroker.PublishAsync("UN/AlternativeProject/AlternativeIIII", testPacketGeneric); var BasicPacketStored = await BasicPacketBroker.RequestAsync("UN/AlternativeProject/AlternativeIIII");
Console.WriteLine("Registered Message: " + BasicPacketStored[0].Name + " Data: " + BasicPacketStored[0].DataAsString());
Console.WriteLine(Environment.NewLine); //Listen test Console.WriteLine("Listening Test..."); await BasicPacketBroker.SubscribeAsync("UN/AlternativeProject/AlternativeIIII", async pkt =&gt;
{ Console.WriteLine( $"Message recieved! " + Environment.NewLine + $"Topic: {pkt.Name}" + Environment.NewLine + $"Data: {pkt.DataAsString()}"); await Task.CompletedTask;
}); await BasicPacketBroker.PublishAsync("UN/AlternativeProject/AlternativeIIII", testPacketGeneric);

Today this is the handshake's admission gate (HandshakeOptions.BannedClients). The design extends it to graded trust that filters what a peer may pull:public bool ShouldSync(Node node, TrustLevel requesterTrust) =&gt; node.MinimumTrust &lt;= requesterTrust &amp;&amp; !node.Blacklisted(requesterId) // ✅ banned-list exists today &amp;&amp; node.VisibilityRules.AllowSync(context); // 📐 public SyncPayload GetFilteredPayload(TrustLevel trust) =&gt; trust switch
{ TrustLevel.Stranger =&gt; PublicDataOnly(), TrustLevel.Acquaintance =&gt; BasicProfile + PublicFiles, TrustLevel.Friend =&gt; MostDataExceptPrivate, TrustLevel.Trusted =&gt; EverythingExceptSecrets, TrustLevel.DeviceOwner =&gt; CompleteSync, _ =&gt; EmptyPayload()
};
Because identity is a Kyber keypair proven during the handshake, trust can be bound to a stable client identity rather than a spoofable address.public enum YuukoSyncMode
{ Realtime, // instant, bidirectional (Zenoh pub/sub) LivePresence, // presence, typing indicators Opportunistic, // when devices are near (BLE) Background, // periodic background sync OnDemand, // user-initiated (today: EclipseBroker.RequestAsync) QRHandshake, // one-time pairing bootstrap Sneakernet, // USB / file transfer EmergencySync, // critical data only TrustBootstrap // initial trust establishment
}
